[CENTER][SIZE=6][B]Evolution X 12.3 — Android 17[/B][/SIZE]
[SIZE=4]Google Pixel 7 Pro (cheetah) | UNOFFICIAL[/SIZE][/CENTER]

[CODE]
/*
 * Your warranty is now void. You flash this at your own risk.
 * I'm not responsible for bricked devices, lost data, or anything else.
 */
[/CODE]

[SIZE=5][B]About[/B][/SIZE]
An [B]unofficial[/B] build of [B]Evolution X 12.3 on Android 17 for the Pixel 7 Pro (cheetah)[/B], built from Evolution X's own Pixel 7 device trees and blobs with LineageOS's gs201 kernel. I also build Evolution X for the Pixel 11 series and the Pixel 8 Pro, and this build gets the same fixes and release checks as those.

The Pixel 7 Pro has an official Evolution X maintainer for Android 16 (nhansp); Evolution X hasn't released official Android 17 builds for it yet. When it does, switch to the official builds. Ours are signed with different keys, so moving between the two needs a clean flash.

[B]For the Pixel 7 Pro (cheetah) only.[/B] Don't flash it on the Pixel 7 (panther) or any other model.

This is [B]not[/B] an official Evolution X build. Please don't report its bugs to the Evolution X team; report them in this thread.

[SIZE=5][B]Build info[/B][/SIZE]
[LIST]
[*]Evolution X 12.3, Android 17, security patch 2026-10-01 (system), 2026-10-05 (vendor)
[*]Vendor blobs and firmware from Google's October release CP3A.261005.002.A1
[*]Build type: user, signed with private release keys, not rooted
[*]Google apps included
[*]Kernel: Linux 6.1.162, Google's signed GKI (android14-6.1) with the Pixel 7 Pro's modules and device tree built from LineageOS's gs-6.1 kernel source (build_kernel, with the ROM)
[*]Over-the-air updates through the built-in Updater (Settings > System > System updates); they include Google's bootloader and radio
[/LIST]

[SIZE=5][B]Tested[/B][/SIZE]
I run this ROM on my own Pixel 7 Pro. This release was installed as an over-the-air update from the previous build, and checked on the phone before release:
[LIST]
[*]Installs as an update and boots (A/B, the inactive slot); root through Magisk's inactive-slot install still works
[*]Mobile network service (T-Mobile eSIM, in service on 5G SA) and Wi-Fi
[*]Adaptive Connectivity (SCONE) runs with its system permissions; no system crashes after boot
[*]Preinstalled apps keep their precompiled code across updates
[/LIST]
Not yet confirmed by me on this build: calls and SMS (including VoLTE), cameras, fingerprint and face unlock, Bluetooth audio, NFC payments, and a clean flash of the fastboot zip (I installed this release as an update). Reports welcome.

[SIZE=5][B]What's in it beyond Evolution X's trees[/B][/SIZE]
[LIST]
[*]Adaptive Connectivity (SCONE) and Google Fi (Tycho) installed as on stock
[*]NFC on after a clean install, as on stock
[*]Google Play services' system updater is turned off, so it can't prepare Google's stock OTAs (on other Pixels that made the first unlock after a reboot ask for the PIN twice)
[*]Private Compute Services isn't cut off from the apps that use it (a leftover restriction stock dropped)
[*]Stock Pixel 7 Pro settings that were missing: the screenshot Edit button opens Markup, Quick Share copy, the Files button, Google Photos in the photo picker, camera pinned in memory for faster launch, stock Wi-Fi switching thresholds on 5 GHz, Google Home in device controls
[*]One Android System Intelligence (the Pixel 7 Pro's build, not a second copy for the Pixel 11)
[*]App odex ships with the apps (in /system, /system_ext and /product) instead of system_other, so preinstalled apps don't lose their precompiled code after each update
[*]The Contactless payments settings page that apps such as Google Wallet open loads again
[/LIST]

[SIZE=5][B]Known issues[/B][/SIZE]
[LIST]
[*]Gemini Nano features aren't available. Google serves its on-device AI models only to supported Pixels with a locked bootloader.
[*]The Extreme Charging tile isn't available on the Pixel 7 Pro.
[/LIST]

[SIZE=5][B]Requirements[/B][/SIZE]
[LIST]
[*]Unlocked bootloader (unlocking wipes your phone)
[*]Google's stock firmware [B]CP3A.261005.002.A1[/B] (October 2026) or newer. The fastboot zip doesn't flash a bootloader or radio, so install the latest Android 17 factory image for the Pixel 7 Pro with the Android Flash Tool first. Updates through the Updater keep the firmware current after that.
[*]Latest platform-tools (adb/fastboot)
[/LIST]

[SIZE=5][B]Installation (clean flash)[/B][/SIZE]
Download the fastboot zip and check its SHA-256, then:
[CODE]
adb reboot bootloader
fastboot -w update EvolutionX-17.0-20261024-cheetah-12.3-Unofficial-fastboot.zip
[/CODE]
[B]-w wipes all your data.[/B] fastboot switches to fastbootd partway through to flash the dynamic partitions; leave the phone plugged in until it reboots by itself (a few minutes). The first boot takes a few minutes more.

[B]Updating:[/B] Settings > System > System updates. It installs in the background, then restart. Rooted? Before restarting, Magisk > Install > [I]Install to inactive slot (after OTA)[/I], or root is gone.

